    <section>
      <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No cookies. Not even a "necessary" one, so there is no cookie banner.</li>
        <li>No third-party requests. Fonts, images and code are all served from this domain - your visit is never disclosed to Google, a CDN, or anyone else.</li>
        <li>No advertising, no fingerprinting, no social media embeds or share-button trackers.</li>
        <li>No accounts, no comments platform, no data sold or shared - there is nothing to sell.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Analytics</h2>
      <p>
        I like to know which posts people find useful, so this site uses
        privacy-preserving analytics measured at the network edge by the host
        (Cloudflare). It counts page views in aggregate - no cookies, no
        client-side tracking script, no personal identifiers, and no cross-site
        tracking - and it never builds a profile of you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The contact form</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/contact/">contact form</a> sends one email, to me, through
        a form handler running on my own infrastructure. Your name, email
        address and message exist in my inbox and nowhere else. I use them only
        to reply to you, and I delete the thread when the conversation is done.
        Nothing is added to a mailing list - if I ever start a newsletter it
        will be strictly opt-in.
      </p>
      <p>
        To keep spam off the site and out of my inbox, when you send the contact
        form or post a comment, your IP address may be checked against
        <a href="https://www.projecthoneypot.org/" rel="noopener">Project Honeypot</a>,
        a shared reputation list of known spam sources. Any such check runs on my
        server, never in your browser; it sets no cookie, and I don't store the
        result - it only helps me tell a real person from a spambot.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Server logs</h2>
      <p>
        Like every web host, mine keeps short-lived technical logs to detect
        abuse and keep the site running. These are handled by the hosting
        provider (Cloudflare); IP addresses are not retained long-term, and the
        logs are not used to track or profile visitors.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Links elsewhere</h2>
      <p>
        When a post links to another site, that site's own privacy practices
        apply from the moment you click. I link to privacy-respecting places
        where a choice exists.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Your rights, and questions</h2>
      <p>
        Under UK GDPR you can ask me what I hold about you (almost certainly: at
        most, an email you sent me), ask me to correct it, or ask me to delete
        it. Just <a href="/contact/">drop me a line</a> - no form, no process,
        I'll just do it.
      </p>
    </section>
